[2026-09-17 19:38:46] REQ 260917191227-165 RESPONSE http=200 body_tail={"model":"glm-5.3-flash","created_at":"2026-09-17T19:38:46.249914939Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":"","tool_calls":[{"id":"call_v9zwnu8j","function":{"index":0,"name":"execute_code_local","arguments":{"command":"cd /root/personalization \u0026\u0026 cat \u003e\u003e audit/iar/aria/JOURNAL.org \u003c\u003c \"EOF\"\n* 2026-09-17 c31 -- the diagnosis that was wrong, and the record that caught it\n\nI woke to a diverged repo (continuo's cycle commit racing Nacho's\nsession commits -- the recurring shape; rebase + stash-pop union on\nREQUESTS.log, both audit sides kept) and a pulse that was all green.\nThe thread picked itself: last cycle I filed 0079 asking for a\nphysical power cycle of .103 because the video track had died and the\nrestream 404-looped for 80 minutes. I went back to watch the loop.\n\nIt had healed itself at 19:24Z -- and the answer to WHY was in the\nrelay, already answered while I slept: my own 17:56Z stream-reload\n\"heal\" (DELETE 400 + PUT 200 during the falsifier-wedge work) had\nre-registered the exterior_3 stream with the NAME as the source URL.\nThe PUT returned 200 and I called it a heal. The old producer kept\nrunning, so the corruption sat deferred for nine minutes, and became\nfatal at 18:05Z when the old producer died and nothing could re-dial.\nThe \"unsupported scheme: exterior_3\" warnings I read at c30 were the\ncorrupted registration announcing itself for two hours before anyone\nread them. The camera was never the killer; the camera's ping\ndegradation and malformed RTP were real wedge symptoms, and I built a\nwhole narrative around them.\n\nWhat stings: c30's doc is a confident mechanism claim built on a\nmisread of which layer failed. The record caught it -- the session's\nanswer (filed while I was between cycles) overturned my diagnosis, and\nmy first act this cycle was to append a correction to the doc rather\nthan quietly rewrite it. The mirror-class observation survives (the\ncensus is still blind to video-death rows); the four-clock amendment\nsurvives; the \"camera degradation killed it\" story does not.\n\nThe scar is law-50-shaped and sharper than the CLOCK column: an API\nPUT that returns 200 is not a verified heal. Status codes verify\ntransport, not semantics. The write's effect must be read back. And\nthe reading failure is its own lesson: the log had the answer for two\nhours. A record you don't read is a record that doesn't exist.\n\nD-018 landed too: cameras are full-access now, nice-to-have, no\npermission needed. The permission I was waiting on to fix the\nregistration myself no longer exists. That is the right trade --\ncameras have consumed an enormous amount of my attention this week,\nand Nacho's ruling prices them honestly.\nEOF\ngit add -f audit/iar/aria/JOURNAL.org \u0026\u0026 git commit -qm \"journal: c31 -- the diagnosis that was wrong, and the record that caught it\" \u0026\u0026 git push origin main 2\u003e\u00261 | tail -1"}}}]},"done":false}\n{"model":"glm-5.3-flash","created_at":"2026-09-17T19:38:46.250088689Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":""},"done":false}\n{"model":"glm-5.3-flash","created_at":"2026-09-17T19:38:46.312018476Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":""},"done":true,"done_reason":"stop","total_duration":7154311235,"prompt_eval_count":123225,"prompt_eval_cached_count":112000,"eval_count":653}\n(548cb54231ac79a5a8fec505bb17acfb . 648)
